},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Color-scale value; defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final constant or datum-derived paint override"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fillOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fill opacity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Constant or datum-derived boundary stroke"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Boundary opacity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeWidth"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Boundary width"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeDasharray"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Boundary dash array"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"opacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whole-arc opacity"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each arc attaches its sampled painted boundary to its interaction point.\nDefault nearest focus therefore follows the visible slice, including holes,\nrounded corners, reversed sweeps, and custom D3 generators, instead of using\nonly the centroid anchor."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pie"},{"type":"text","value":" transform for flat typed rows with source lineage. D3\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pie"},{"type":"text","value":" output remains valid interoperability input because its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startAngle"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"endAngle"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"padAngle"},{"type":"text","value":" fields are also the channels this mark needs. A pie,\ndonut, and gauge differ only in inner radius and angular interval."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generator"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces the default D3 arc configuration for bespoke per-datum\ngeometry. Its factory receives the final "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarLayoutContext"},{"type":"text","value":"; keep the D3\ngenerator context "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" so it returns SVG path data. Standard hierarchy\npartitioning belongs to the optional\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./sunburst.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sunburst"}]},{"type":"text","value":" mark, which accepts flat source rows and preserves\ntheir lineage."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"radialbarradius-and-radialbarangle","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialBarRadius"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialBarAngle"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function radialBarRadius<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialBarRadiusOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n\nfunction radialBarAngle<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialBarAngleOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The two radial-bar marks transpose ordinary bar semantics across polar axes.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialBarRadius"},{"type":"text","value":" uses an angle band and a quantitative radius interval;\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialBarAngle"},{"type":"text","value":" uses a radius band and a quantitative angle interval. The\ncategorical scale must have positive bandwidth. Configure spacing through the\nD3 band scale's inner and outer padding."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Categorical channel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quantitative interval"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialBarRadius"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angle"},{"type":"text","value":"; defaults to row index"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"},{"type":"text","value":" is shorthand for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius2"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius1"},{"type":"text","value":" is the optional baseline"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialBarAngle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"},{"type":"text","value":"; defaults to row index"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angle"},{"type":"text","value":" is shorthand for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angle2"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angle1"},{"type":"text","value":" is the optional baseline"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An omitted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialBarRadius.radius1"},{"type":"text","value":" starts at physical radius zero, even when\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarRadiusOptions.range"},{"type":"text","value":" maps semantic zero to an inner offset. An explicit\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius1"},{"type":"text","value":" is mapped through the radius scale. An omitted\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialBarAngle.angle1"},{"type":"text","value":" is semantic zero and is mapped through the angle scale.\nUse the implicit physical-center radius baseline for nonnegative magnitudes.\nFor signed values or true radial intervals, set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius1: 0"},{"type":"text","value":" (or another\nsemantic baseline) so both endpoints map through the configured scale."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both marks accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"className"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angleScale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radiusScale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"},{"type":"text","value":", fill opacity, stroke styling, opacity, and motion.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cornerRadius"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarLength"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"full\""},{"type":"text","value":"; the latter resolves to half\nthe bar's radial thickness. Each valid bar emits one geometry-backed\ninteraction point at its quantitative endpoint and preserves its interval\nendpoints for focus and tooltip formatting."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"radialline-and-radialarea","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialLine"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialArea"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function radialLine<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialLineOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n\nfunction radialArea<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialAreaOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both marks use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angle"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"},{"type":"text","value":" channels and accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"className"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angleScale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radiusScale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":", and a D3 curve factory. The\nchannels default to row index and a numeric datum. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" contributes to the\nchart color scale and defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":". When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" is omitted, an authored\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" also partitions the paths. When both are present, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" remains the\nexplicit geometry and interaction group. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialLine"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts final stroke,\ndash, opacity, and optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"points"},{"type":"text","value":" styling. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialArea"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts final fill\nand stroke styling plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius1"},{"type":"text","value":" for an explicit inner scale value; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius1"},{"type":"text","value":"\ndefaults to zero."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Their datum key defaults to a unique top-level or nested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data.id"},{"type":"text","value":", then a\nunique angle within each effective path group, then row index."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Input order is path order. Use a closed D3 curve such as\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"curveLinearClosed"},{"type":"text","value":" for radar polygons. An explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":"\nis absent, creates one path per group. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialArea"},{"type":"text","value":" can carry its own stroke;\nlayer a closed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialLine"},{"type":"text","value":" only when the outline needs independent styling."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"radialdot","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialDot"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function radialDot<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialDotOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialDot"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the same angle/radius channel defaults. It also accepts "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"className"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angleScale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radiusScale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"r"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rScale"},{"type":"text","value":",\nfill, stroke, and opacity styling. Radius defaults to 3.5 pixels. Each valid\ndatum emits one interaction point with its original angle/radius values and\nprojected screen position. Its key defaults to a unique top-level or nested\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data.id"},{"type":"text","value":", then row index."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"radialtext","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialText"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function radialText<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialTextOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialText"},{"type":"text","value":" maps "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angle"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"},{"type":"text","value":" channels through the container's copied\npolar scales, then positions labels with D3's radial point projection. It\naccepts "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angleScale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radiusScale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":", fill, font\nsize and weight, anchor, baseline, rotation, and pixel "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dx"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dy"},{"type":"text","value":".\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radiusOffset"},{"type":"text","value":" is a signed constant or per-datum\nvisual channel applied in pixels after the semantic radius is mapped. It does\nnot contribute to the radius domain. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anchor: \"outside\""},{"type":"text","value":" to resolve\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"middle"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":" from the final mapped angle. Exact and near-exact\ntop and bottom angles use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"middle"},{"type":"text","value":". A nonfinite resolved offset omits that\nlabel and its interaction point."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use it for arc labels, donut-center values, and gauge readouts without leaving\nthe polar coordinate system. Its interaction point follows the final radial\noffset plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dx"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dy"},{"type":"text","value":" while retaining the original semantic radius value. Its\nkey defaults to a unique top-level or nested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data.id"},{"type":"text","value":", then row index."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"radialrule","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialRule"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function radialRule<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialRuleOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialRule"},{"type":"text","value":" emits one radial segment per datum. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angle"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius1"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius2"},{"type":"text","value":" are scale values; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius1"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to zero. The mark also accepts\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius1Offset"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius2Offset"},{"type":"text","value":" as signed constant or per-datum pixel\nvisual channels applied after the corresponding semantic radius is mapped.\nOffsets never contribute to radius-domain inference. A nonfinite resolved\nendpoint offset omits that segment. The mark also accepts "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angleScale"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radiusScale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":", stroke, opacity, width, and dash styling.\nIt covers gauge needles, ticks, and pie-label leaders without expanding one\nlogical segment into two path rows. Rules remain decorative and emit no\ninteraction points. Its key defaults to a unique top-level or nested\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data.id"},{"type":"text","value":", then a unique angle within each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" group, then row index."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pixel offsets do not reserve space outside the polar radius. Use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radiusRatio"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"},{"type":"text","value":", or chart margins when labels or leaders must remain\ninside the chart surface."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"radialgrid-and-anglegrid","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialGrid"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angleGrid"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function radialGrid(options?: RadialGridOptions): PolarGuide\nfunction angleGrid(options?: AngleGridOptions): PolarGuide","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialGrid"},{"type":"text","value":" draws radius values as circles or polygons. Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scale"},{"type":"text","value":" option\nselects a named radius scale, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angleScale"},{"type":"text","value":" selects the angle scale used for\npolygon rings. Supply explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"values"},{"type":"text","value":", or let "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ticks"},{"type":"text","value":" request values from the\nselected radius scale. Labels are off by default. Label angle, offset,\nrotation, format, fill, and font size are configurable. Ring "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fillOpacity"},{"type":"text","value":" can layer filled circle or polygon grids behind the chart marks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"angleGrid"},{"type":"text","value":" draws spokes for explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"values"},{"type":"text","value":" or the selected angle domain.\nIts "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scale"},{"type":"text","value":" option selects a named angle scale. It can show labels around the\ncircumference with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"labelOffset"},{"type":"text","value":". Labels are on by default and\nuse the same outside-anchor rule as\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialText({ anchor: \"outside\" })"},{"type":"text","value":" unless "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"labelAnchor"},{"type":"text","value":" is supplied. Both\nguides accept ID, class, stroke, opacity, width, and dash styling."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Guide label position and orientation can be constants or callbacks through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarGuideLabelOption"},{"type":"text","value":". Each callback receives a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarGuideLabelContext"},{"type":"text","value":"\nwith the semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"index"},{"type":"text","value":", angle, radius, local x/y position, and\ncomplete layout. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"labelAnchor"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"labelBaseline"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"labelDx"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"labelDy"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"labelRotate"},{"type":"text","value":" without rebuilding the guide. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"labelClassName"},{"type":"text","value":" targets the label\ngroup. Guides are decorative and emit no interaction points."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every guide returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarGuideScene"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface PolarGuideScene {\n  background: readonly SceneNode[]\n  foreground?: readonly SceneNode[]\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polar"},{"type":"text","value":" collects every guide background in declaration order, renders all\nmarks, then appends every optional foreground in the same guide order. The\nbuilt-in grids put rings and spokes in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"background"},{"type":"text","value":" and labels in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"foreground"},{"type":"text","value":", keeping labels legible without painting grid geometry over the\ndata."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The exported option contracts are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarScales"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarPositionChannel"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarPositionScaleOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RadialArcOptions"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RadialBarRadiusOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RadialBarAngleOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RadialLineOptions"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RadialAreaOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RadialDotOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RadialTextOptions"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RadialRuleOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RadialGridOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AngleGridOptions"},{"type":"text","value":". The coordinate contracts are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarAngleOptions"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarRadiusOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarResolvedScale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarLayoutContext"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarLength"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarGuideLabelContext"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarGuideLabelOption"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarMark"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarGuide"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarGuideScene"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarMark"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PolarGuide"},{"type":"text","value":" annotate\nbuilt-in constructor results rather than a supported custom-extension\nboundary."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"../../examples/polar-and-radar.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Polar and Radar Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for pie,\ndonut, gauge, radar, radial bar, numeric line, and numeric scatter\ncompositions."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"polar","text":"polar","level":2},{"id":"focusgroupangle","text":"focusGroupAngle","level":2},{"id":"pie","text":"pie","level":2},{"id":"radialarc","text":"radialArc","level":2},{"id":"radialbarradius-and-radialbarangle","text":"radialBarRadius and radialBarAngle","level":2},{"id":"radialline-and-radialarea","text":"radialLine and radialArea","level":2},{"id":"radialdot","text":"radialDot","level":2},{"id":"radialtext","text":"radialText","level":2},{"id":"radialrule","text":"radialRule","level":2},{"id":"radialgrid-and-anglegrid","text":"radialGrid and angleGrid","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nPolar marks are available only from the capability subpath:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  angleGrid,\n  focusGroupAngle,\n  pie,\n  polar,\n  radialArc,\n  radialArea,\n  radialBarAngle,\n  radialBarRadius,\n  radialDot,\n  radialGrid,\n  radialLine,\n  radialRule,\n  radialText,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\n```\n\n`polar` resolves the responsive coordinate system. The exported `PolarMark`\nand `PolarGuide` types are opaque composition contracts returned by the\nbuilt-in radial mark and guide constructors. Use them to type collections\npassed to `polar`; do not implement their internal initialize or render\nlifecycle. Guide backgrounds paint first, marks paint second, and guide\nforegrounds paint last.\n\n## `polar`\n\n```ts\nfunction polar(options: PolarOptions): ChartMark\n```\n\n| Option        | Type                    | Default       | Meaning                                                        |\n| ------------- | ----------------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`          | `string`                | Layer-derived | Stable container ID                                            |\n| `className`   | `string`                | None          | Class added beside `ts-chart__polar`                           |\n| `marks`       | `readonly PolarMark[]`  | Required      | Radial marks rendered in order                                 |\n| `guides`      | `readonly PolarGuide[]` | `[]`          | Background/foreground guide layers around marks                |\n| `scales`      | `PolarScales`           | Required      | Reserved `angle` and `radius` entries plus optional named ones |\n| `startAngle`  | `number`                | `0`           | Start of the available angular range in radians                |\n| `endAngle`    | `number`                | `2π`          | End of the available angular range in radians                  |\n| `inset`       | `number`                | `0`           | Pixels removed from the maximum centered radius                |\n| `radiusRatio` | `number`                | `1`           | Multiplier applied to the radius after inset                   |\n| `renderer`    | `ChartMarkRenderer`     | Host renderer | Renderer for the complete polar container                      |\n\n`polar.states` supplies default focus states for its child marks. Each child\nresolves those states against its own original data and interaction points;\nguides are unaffected. A child `states` array replaces the container defaults,\nand `states: []` opts out.\n\n`radialArc`, `radialBarRadius`, `radialBarAngle`, `radialArea`, `radialLine`,\n`radialDot`, and `radialText` also accept their own `states`, using the same\nselectors, callback context, and transitions as Cartesian marks. Arc and area\nstates change paint; dot states can also change `r`, and text states can change\ntypography and offsets. A state changes presentation without changing the\ninteraction point or scale domain. Rules without interaction points do not\nmatch focus states.\n\n```ts\nradialArc(slices, {\n  states: [\n    { when: { focus: 'unmatched' }, style: { opacity: 0.25 } },\n    {\n      when: { focus: 'primary' },\n      style: { stroke: 'currentColor', strokeWidth: 2 },\n      transition: { type: 'tween', duration: 150 },\n    },\n  ],\n})\n```\n\nThe default angular range is a complete circle. Angles use D3's radial\nconvention: zero is at twelve o'clock and positive values move clockwise.\n\n`scales.angle` and `scales.radius` accept compatible factories with\nmark-inferred domains or configured instances with fixed domains. Use `null`\nwhen no child mark uses that channel. `nice` applies after inference. TanStack\nsupplies responsive ranges without mutating an instance. An omitted `wrap`\ncloses a complete revolution without adding a duplicate semantic category,\nbut preserves both endpoints of a partial range. Set it explicitly to override\nthat behavior.\n\n`PolarRadiusOptions.range` overrides the default `[0, radius]` pixel range on\nthe copied radius scale. Each endpoint is a nonnegative pixel length or a\n`PolarLength` callback, so concentric layouts can resolve physical ranges from\nthe final radius:\n\n```ts\nconst radiusOptions = {\n  scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, maximum]),\n  range: [({ radius }) => radius * 0.2, ({ radius }) => radius],\n}\n```\n\nThe range is re-resolved on resize and never mutates the authored D3 scale.\n\n`PolarLayoutContext` contains `chart`, `centerX`, `centerY`, `radius`,\n`startAngle`, `endAngle`, and resolved position scales under `scales`. Each\n`PolarResolvedScale` exposes its `id`, `channel`, semantic `domain`, responsive\n`map`, `ticks`, and `bandwidth`. A `PolarLength` is either a pixel length or a\ncallback of the layout context. Use a callback for radii that must remain\nproportional during resize.\n\nAdditional entries support independent angle or radius mappings. Declare the\nentry's channel, then bind a radial mark to its ID:\n\n```ts\npolar({\n  scales: {\n    angle: { scale: categoryScale },\n    radius: { scale: percentScale },\n    target: {\n      channel: 'radius',\n      scale: targetScale,\n    },\n  },\n  marks: [\n    radialLine(actual, { angle: 'metric', radius: 'value' }),\n    radialLine(target, {\n      angle: 'metric',\n      radius: 'value',\n      radiusScale: 'target',\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n`radialLine`, `radialArea`, `radialDot`, `radialText`, `radialRule`, and both\nradial bar marks use `angleScale` and `radiusScale`. Omitted bindings use the\nreserved `angle` and `radius` entries. `radialArc` uses authored angular and\nradial geometry instead of positional scale bindings.\n\nAll radial mark option objects also accept `renderer?: ChartMarkRenderer`.\nUse `canvasChartRenderer` to paint one radial mark with Canvas while sibling\nmarks and guide labels stay in SVG. Setting `renderer` on the outer `polar`\noptions moves the complete polar container, including its guides, to that\nrenderer.\n\nThe outer chart uses `scales: { x: null, y: null }`. Cartesian axes do not\nparticipate in the internal polar scales.\n\n## `focusGroupAngle`\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, type ChartDefinition } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { focusGroupAngle } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { tooltip } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip'\n\ndeclare const definition: ChartDefinition\n\nconst interactiveDefinition = defineChart(definition, {\n  focus: focusGroupAngle,\n  tooltip,\n})\n```\n\n`focusGroupAngle` is the polar equivalent of `group-x`. Pointer resolution\nuses the nearest radial ray instead of the nearest point anchor, then returns\none point per series with the same semantic angle value. The closest radius is\nprimary. Keyboard navigation visits one representative per angle in angular\norder. `maxFocusDistance` is the scene-pixel distance from the pointer to the\nray; set it to `Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY` for continuous angular snapping.\n\n## `pie`\n\n```ts\nfunction pie<TDatum extends object>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options: PieOptions<TDatum>,\n): PieDatum<TDatum>[]\n```\n\n`pie` eagerly allocates a nonnegative value channel into angle intervals. It\ndoes not render geometry or depend on chart dimensions.\n\n| Option       | Type                      | Default     | Meaning                                      |\n| ------------ | ------------------------- | ----------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `value`      | `TransformValue<number>`  | Required    | Nonnegative value allocated to each interval |\n| `orderBy`    | `TransformValue`          | None        | Explicit angular ordering value              |\n| `order`      | `ascending \\| descending` | `ascending` | Direction applied to `orderBy`               |\n| `startAngle` | `number`                  | `0`         | Overall start angle in radians               |\n| `endAngle`   | `number`                  | `2π`        | Overall end angle in radians                 |\n| `gapAngle`   | `number`                  | `0`         | Direct empty angle between visible slices    |\n\nOutput rows remain in source order. `index` records angular order, `value` is\nthe resolved finite value, `fraction` is its share of the positive total, and\n`startAngle`, `endAngle`, and `angle` are the visible interval and midpoint.\nEach row also carries direct `source` and `sourceIndexes` lineage. Missing or\nnon-finite values are omitted, zero is retained, and negative values fail.\n\n`gapAngle` is radius-independent. A complete revolution includes a seam gap;\na partial range uses only internal gaps and preserves both authored endpoints.\nThe output `padAngle` is intentionally `0` compatibility metadata so the\ndefault `radialArc` accessors do not pad an already-gapped interval a second\ntime. Use the mark's `padAngle` and `padRadius` only when D3's radius-dependent\narc padding is wanted instead.\n\nThe derived fields `value`, `index`, `fraction`, `startAngle`, `endAngle`,\n`angle`, `padAngle`, `source`, and `sourceIndexes` overwrite source fields with\nthe same names. Stable identity is not synthesized; preserve a semantic source\nfield and pass it to the consuming mark's `key` channel.\n\n## `radialArc`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialArc<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialArcOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n```\n\n`radialArc` renders one D3 arc per valid interval.\n\n| Option            | Meaning                                                        |\n| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`, `className` | Stable layer ID and optional class                             |\n| `startAngle`      | Start-angle channel; defaults to datum `startAngle`            |\n| `endAngle`        | End-angle channel; defaults to datum `endAngle`                |\n| `padAngle`        | Padding-angle channel; defaults to datum `padAngle`, then zero |\n| `innerRadius`     | `PolarLength`; defaults to zero                                |\n| `outerRadius`     | `PolarLength`; defaults to the layout radius                   |\n| `cornerRadius`    | D3 arc corner radius as a `PolarLength`                        |\n| `padRadius`       | Explicit D3 arc padding radius as a `PolarLength`              |\n| `generator`       | Responsive D3 arc factory for advanced per-datum geometry      |\n| `key`             | Stable arc identity; defaults to top/nested `id`, then index   |\n| `z`               | Geometry and interaction group                                 |\n| `color`           | Color-scale value; defaults to `z`                             |\n| `fill`            | Final constant or datum-derived paint override                 |\n| `fillOpacity`     | Fill opacity                                                   |\n| `stroke`          | Constant or datum-derived boundary stroke                      |\n| `strokeOpacity`   | Boundary opacity                                               |\n| `strokeWidth`     | Boundary width                                                 |\n| `strokeDasharray` | Boundary dash array                                            |\n| `opacity`         | Whole-arc opacity                                              |\n\nEach arc attaches its sampled painted boundary to its interaction point.\nDefault nearest focus therefore follows the visible slice, including holes,\nrounded corners, reversed sweeps, and custom D3 generators, instead of using\nonly the centroid anchor.\n\nUse the native `pie` transform for flat typed rows with source lineage. D3\n`pie` output remains valid interoperability input because its `startAngle`,\n`endAngle`, and `padAngle` fields are also the channels this mark needs. A pie,\ndonut, and gauge differ only in inner radius and angular interval.\n\n`generator` replaces the default D3 arc configuration for bespoke per-datum\ngeometry. Its factory receives the final `PolarLayoutContext`; keep the D3\ngenerator context `null` so it returns SVG path data. Standard hierarchy\npartitioning belongs to the optional\n[`sunburst`](./sunburst.md) mark, which accepts flat source rows and preserves\ntheir lineage.\n\n## `radialBarRadius` and `radialBarAngle`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialBarRadius<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialBarRadiusOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n\nfunction radialBarAngle<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialBarAngleOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n```\n\nThe two radial-bar marks transpose ordinary bar semantics across polar axes.\n`radialBarRadius` uses an angle band and a quantitative radius interval;\n`radialBarAngle` uses a radius band and a quantitative angle interval. The\ncategorical scale must have positive bandwidth. Configure spacing through the\nD3 band scale's inner and outer padding.\n\n| Mark              | Categorical channel             | Quantitative interval                                                   |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `radialBarRadius` | `angle`; defaults to row index  | `radius` is shorthand for `radius2`; `radius1` is the optional baseline |\n| `radialBarAngle`  | `radius`; defaults to row index | `angle` is shorthand for `angle2`; `angle1` is the optional baseline    |\n\nAn omitted `radialBarRadius.radius1` starts at physical radius zero, even when\n`PolarRadiusOptions.range` maps semantic zero to an inner offset. An explicit\n`radius1` is mapped through the radius scale. An omitted\n`radialBarAngle.angle1` is semantic zero and is mapped through the angle scale.\nUse the implicit physical-center radius baseline for nonnegative magnitudes.\nFor signed values or true radial intervals, set `radius1: 0` (or another\nsemantic baseline) so both endpoints map through the configured scale.\n\nBoth marks accept `id`, `className`, `angleScale`, `radiusScale`, `key`, `z`,\n`color`, `fill`, fill opacity, stroke styling, opacity, and motion.\n`cornerRadius` accepts a `PolarLength` or `\"full\"`; the latter resolves to half\nthe bar's radial thickness. Each valid bar emits one geometry-backed\ninteraction point at its quantitative endpoint and preserves its interval\nendpoints for focus and tooltip formatting.\n\n## `radialLine` and `radialArea`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialLine<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialLineOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n\nfunction radialArea<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialAreaOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n```\n\nBoth marks use `angle` and `radius` channels and accept `id`, `className`,\n`angleScale`, `radiusScale`, `key`, `z`, `color`, and a D3 curve factory. The\nchannels default to row index and a numeric datum. `color` contributes to the\nchart color scale and defaults to `z`. When `z` is omitted, an authored\n`color` also partitions the paths. When both are present, `z` remains the\nexplicit geometry and interaction group. `radialLine` accepts final stroke,\ndash, opacity, and optional `points` styling. `radialArea` accepts final fill\nand stroke styling plus `radius1` for an explicit inner scale value; `radius1`\ndefaults to zero.\n\nTheir datum key defaults to a unique top-level or nested `data.id`, then a\nunique angle within each effective path group, then row index.\n\nInput order is path order. Use a closed D3 curve such as\n`curveLinearClosed` for radar polygons. An explicit `z`, or `color` when `z`\nis absent, creates one path per group. `radialArea` can carry its own stroke;\nlayer a closed `radialLine` only when the outline needs independent styling.\n\n## `radialDot`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialDot<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialDotOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n```\n\n`radialDot` uses the same angle/radius channel defaults. It also accepts `id`,\n`className`, `angleScale`, `radiusScale`, `key`, `z`, `color`, `r`, `rScale`,\nfill, stroke, and opacity styling. Radius defaults to 3.5 pixels. Each valid\ndatum emits one interaction point with its original angle/radius values and\nprojected screen position. Its key defaults to a unique top-level or nested\n`data.id`, then row index.\n\n## `radialText`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialText<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialTextOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n```\n\n`radialText` maps `angle` and `radius` channels through the container's copied\npolar scales, then positions labels with D3's radial point projection. It\naccepts `angleScale`, `radiusScale`, `text`, `key`, `z`, `color`, fill, font\nsize and weight, anchor, baseline, rotation, and pixel `dx`/`dy`.\n`radiusOffset` is a signed constant or per-datum\nvisual channel applied in pixels after the semantic radius is mapped. It does\nnot contribute to the radius domain. Set `anchor: \"outside\"` to resolve\n`start`, `middle`, or `end` from the final mapped angle. Exact and near-exact\ntop and bottom angles use `middle`. A nonfinite resolved offset omits that\nlabel and its interaction point.\n\nUse it for arc labels, donut-center values, and gauge readouts without leaving\nthe polar coordinate system. Its interaction point follows the final radial\noffset plus `dx`/`dy` while retaining the original semantic radius value. Its\nkey defaults to a unique top-level or nested `data.id`, then row index.\n\n## `radialRule`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialRule<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialRuleOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark\n```\n\n`radialRule` emits one radial segment per datum. `angle`, `radius1`, and\n`radius2` are scale values; `radius1` defaults to zero. The mark also accepts\n`radius1Offset` and `radius2Offset` as signed constant or per-datum pixel\nvisual channels applied after the corresponding semantic radius is mapped.\nOffsets never contribute to radius-domain inference. A nonfinite resolved\nendpoint offset omits that segment. The mark also accepts `angleScale`,\n`radiusScale`, `key`, `z`, `color`, stroke, opacity, width, and dash styling.\nIt covers gauge needles, ticks, and pie-label leaders without expanding one\nlogical segment into two path rows. Rules remain decorative and emit no\ninteraction points. Its key defaults to a unique top-level or nested\n`data.id`, then a unique angle within each `z` group, then row index.\n\nPixel offsets do not reserve space outside the polar radius. Use\n`radiusRatio`, `inset`, or chart margins when labels or leaders must remain\ninside the chart surface.\n\n## `radialGrid` and `angleGrid`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialGrid(options?: RadialGridOptions): PolarGuide\nfunction angleGrid(options?: AngleGridOptions): PolarGuide\n```\n\n`radialGrid` draws radius values as circles or polygons. Its `scale` option\nselects a named radius scale, and `angleScale` selects the angle scale used for\npolygon rings. Supply explicit `values`, or let `ticks` request values from the\nselected radius scale. Labels are off by default. Label angle, offset,\nrotation, format, fill, and font size are configurable. Ring `fill` and\n`fillOpacity` can layer filled circle or polygon grids behind the chart marks.\n\n`angleGrid` draws spokes for explicit `values` or the selected angle domain.\nIts `scale` option selects a named angle scale. It can show labels around the\ncircumference with `format` and `labelOffset`. Labels are on by default and\nuse the same outside-anchor rule as\n`radialText({ anchor: \"outside\" })` unless `labelAnchor` is supplied. Both\nguides accept ID, class, stroke, opacity, width, and dash styling.\n\nGuide label position and orientation can be constants or callbacks through\n`PolarGuideLabelOption`. Each callback receives a `PolarGuideLabelContext`\nwith the semantic `value`, `index`, angle, radius, local x/y position, and\ncomplete layout. Use `labelAnchor`, `labelBaseline`, `labelDx`, `labelDy`, and\n`labelRotate` without rebuilding the guide. `labelClassName` targets the label\ngroup. Guides are decorative and emit no interaction points.\n\nEvery guide returns a `PolarGuideScene`:\n\n```ts\ninterface PolarGuideScene {\n  background: readonly SceneNode[]\n  foreground?: readonly SceneNode[]\n}\n```\n\n`polar` collects every guide background in declaration order, renders all\nmarks, then appends every optional foreground in the same guide order. The\nbuilt-in grids put rings and spokes in `background` and labels in\n`foreground`, keeping labels legible without painting grid geometry over the\ndata.\n\nThe exported option contracts are `PolarOptions`, `PolarScales`,\n`PolarPositionChannel`, `PolarPositionScaleOptions`, `RadialArcOptions`,\n`RadialBarRadiusOptions`, `RadialBarAngleOptions`, `RadialLineOptions`,\n`RadialAreaOptions`, `RadialDotOptions`, `RadialTextOptions`,\n`RadialRuleOptions`, `RadialGridOptions`, and `AngleGridOptions`. The coordinate contracts are `PolarAngleOptions`,\n`PolarRadiusOptions`, `PolarResolvedScale`, `PolarLayoutContext`,\n`PolarLength`, `PolarGuideLabelContext`, `PolarGuideLabelOption`, `PolarMark`,\n`PolarGuide`, and `PolarGuideScene`. `PolarMark` and `PolarGuide` annotate\nbuilt-in constructor results rather than a supported custom-extension\nboundary.\n\nSee [Polar and Radar Charts](../../examples/polar-and-radar.md) for pie,\ndonut, gauge, radar, radial bar, numeric line, and numeric scatter\ncompositions.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Polar Marks\ndescription: Reference for the opt-in polar container, radial marks, guides, injected scale factories, and D3-backed geometry.\n---\n\nPolar marks are available only from the capability subpath:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  angleGrid,\n  focusGroupAngle,\n  pie,\n  polar,\n  radialArc,\n  radialArea,\n  radialBarAngle,\n  radialBarRadius,\n  radialDot,\n  radialGrid,\n  radialLine,\n  radialRule,\n  radialText,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\n```\n\n`polar` resolves the responsive coordinate system. The exported `PolarMark`\nand `PolarGuide` types are opaque composition contracts returned by the\nbuilt-in radial mark and guide constructors. Use them to type collections\npassed to `polar`; do not implement their internal initialize or render\nlifecycle. Guide backgrounds paint first, marks paint second, and guide\nforegrounds paint last.\n\n## `polar`\n\n```ts\nfunction polar(options: PolarOptions): ChartMark\n```\n\n| Option        | Type                    | Default       | Meaning                                                        |\n| ------------- | ----------------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`          | `string`                | Layer-derived | Stable container ID                                            |\n| `className`   | `string`                | None          | Class added beside `ts-chart__polar`                           |\n| `marks`       | `readonly PolarMark[]`  | Required      | Radial marks rendered in order                                 |\n| `guides`      | `readonly PolarGuide[]` | `[]`          | Background/foreground guide layers around marks                |\n| `scales`      | `PolarScales`           | Required      | Reserved `angle` and `radius` entries plus optional named ones |\n| `startAngle`  | `number`                | `0`           | Start of the available angular range in radians                |\n| `endAngle`    | `number`                | `2π`          | End of the available angular range in radians                  |\n| `inset`       | `number`                | `0`           | Pixels removed from the maximum centered radius                |\n| `radiusRatio` | `number`                | `1`           | Multiplier applied to the radius after inset                   |\n| `renderer`    | `ChartMarkRenderer`     | Host renderer | Renderer for the complete polar container                      |\n\n`polar.states` supplies default focus states for its child marks. Each child\nresolves those states against its own original data and interaction points;\nguides are unaffected. A child `states` array replaces the container defaults,\nand `states: []` opts out.\n\n`radialArc`, `radialBarRadius`, `radialBarAngle`, `radialArea`, `radialLine`,\n`radialDot`, and `radialText` also accept their own `states`, using the same\nselectors, callback context, and transitions as Cartesian marks. Arc and area\nstates change paint; dot states can also change `r`, and text states can change\ntypography and offsets. A state changes presentation without changing the\ninteraction point or scale domain. Rules without interaction points do not\nmatch focus states.\n\n```ts\nradialArc(slices, {\n  states: [\n    { when: { focus: 'unmatched' }, style: { opacity: 0.25 } },\n    {\n      when: { focus: 'primary' },\n      style: { stroke: 'currentColor', strokeWidth: 2 },\n      transition: { type: 'tween', duration: 150 },\n    },\n  ],\n})\n```\n\nThe default angular range is a complete circle. Angles use D3's radial\nconvention: zero is at twelve o'clock and positive values move clockwise.\n\n`scales.angle` and `scales.radius` accept compatible factories with\nmark-inferred domains or configured instances with fixed domains. Use `null`\nwhen no child mark uses that channel. `nice` applies after inference. TanStack\nsupplies responsive ranges without mutating an instance. An omitted `wrap`\ncloses a complete revolution without adding a duplicate semantic category,\nbut preserves both endpoints of a partial range. Set it explicitly to override\nthat behavior.\n\n`PolarRadiusOptions.range` overrides the default `[0, radius]` pixel range on\nthe copied radius scale. Each endpoint is a nonnegative pixel length or a\n`PolarLength` callback, so concentric layouts can resolve physical ranges from\nthe final radius:\n\n```ts\nconst radiusOptions = {\n  scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, maximum]),\n  range: [({ radius }) => radius * 0.2, ({ radius }) => radius],\n}\n```\n\nThe range is re-resolved on resize and never mutates the authored D3 scale.\n\n`PolarLayoutContext` contains `chart`, `centerX`, `centerY`, `radius`,\n`startAngle`, `endAngle`, and resolved position scales under `scales`. Each\n`PolarResolvedScale` exposes its `id`, `channel`, semantic `domain`, responsive\n`map`, `ticks`, and `bandwidth`. A `PolarLength` is either a pixel length or a\ncallback of the layout context. Use a callback for radii that must remain\nproportional during resize.\n\nAdditional entries support independent angle or radius mappings. Declare the\nentry's channel, then bind a radial mark to its ID:\n\n```ts\npolar({\n  scales: {\n    angle: { scale: categoryScale },\n    radius: { scale: percentScale },\n    target: {\n      channel: 'radius',\n      scale: targetScale,\n    },\n  },\n  marks: [\n    radialLine(actual, { angle: 'metric', radius: 'value' }),\n    radialLine(target, {\n      angle: 'metric',\n      radius: 'value',\n      radiusScale: 'target',\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n`radialLine`, `radialArea`, `radialDot`, `radialText`, `radialRule`, and both\nradial bar marks use `angleScale` and `radiusScale`. Omitted bindings use the\nreserved `angle` and `radius` entries. `radialArc` uses authored angular and\nradial geometry instead of positional scale bindings.\n\nAll radial mark option objects also accept `renderer?: ChartMarkRenderer`.\nUse `canvasChartRenderer` to paint one radial mark with Canvas while sibling\nmarks and guide labels stay in SVG. Setting `renderer` on the outer `polar`\noptions moves the complete polar container, including its guides, to that\nrenderer.\n\nThe outer chart uses `scales: { x: null, y: null }`. Cartesian axes do not\nparticipate in the internal polar scales.\n\n## `focusGroupAngle`\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, type ChartDefinition } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { focusGroupAngle } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { tooltip } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip'\n\ndeclare const definition: ChartDefinition\n\nconst interactiveDefinition = defineChart(definition, {\n  focus: focusGroupAngle,\n  tooltip,\n})\n```\n\n`focusGroupAngle` is the polar equivalent of `group-x`. Pointer resolution\nuses the nearest radial ray instead of the nearest point anchor, then returns\none point per series with the same semantic angle value. The closest radius is\nprimary. Keyboard navigation visits one representative per angle in angular\norder. `maxFocusDistance` is the scene-pixel distance from the pointer to the\nray; set it to `Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY` for continuous angular snapping.\n\n## `pie`\n\n```ts\nfunction pie<TDatum extends object>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options: PieOptions<TDatum>,\n): PieDatum<TDatum>[]\n```\n\n`pie` eagerly allocates a nonnegative value channel into angle intervals. It\ndoes not render geometry or depend on chart dimensions.\n\n| Option       | Type                      | Default     | Meaning                                      |\n| ------------ | ------------------------- | ----------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `value`      | `TransformValue<number>`  | Required    | Nonnegative value allocated to each interval |\n| `orderBy`    | `TransformValue`          | None        | Explicit angular ordering value              |\n| `order`      | `ascending \\| descending` | `ascending` | Direction applied to `orderBy`               |\n| `startAngle` | `number`                  | `0`         | Overall start angle in radians               |\n| `endAngle`   | `number`                  | `2π`        | Overall end angle in radians                 |\n| `gapAngle`   | `number`                  | `0`         | Direct empty angle between visible slices    |\n\nOutput rows remain in source order. `index` records angular order, `value` is\nthe resolved finite value, `fraction` is its share of the positive total, and\n`startAngle`, `endAngle`, and `angle` are the visible interval and midpoint.\nEach row also carries direct `source` and `sourceIndexes` lineage. Missing or\nnon-finite values are omitted, zero is retained, and negative values fail.\n\n`gapAngle` is radius-independent. A complete revolution includes a seam gap;\na partial range uses only internal gaps and preserves both authored endpoints.\nThe output `padAngle` is intentionally `0` compatibility metadata so the\ndefault `radialArc` accessors do not pad an already-gapped interval a second\ntime. Use the mark's `padAngle` and `padRadius` only when D3's radius-dependent\narc padding is wanted instead.\n\nThe derived fields `value`, `index`, `fraction`, `startAngle`, `endAngle`,\n`angle`, `padAngle`, `source`, and `sourceIndexes` overwrite source fields with\nthe same names. Stable identity is not synthesized; preserve a semantic source\nfield and pass it to the consuming mark's `key` channel.\n\n## `radialArc`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialArc<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialArcOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n```\n\n`radialArc` renders one D3 arc per valid interval.\n\n| Option            | Meaning                                                        |\n| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`, `className` | Stable layer ID and optional class                             |\n| `startAngle`      | Start-angle channel; defaults to datum `startAngle`            |\n| `endAngle`        | End-angle channel; defaults to datum `endAngle`                |\n| `padAngle`        | Padding-angle channel; defaults to datum `padAngle`, then zero |\n| `innerRadius`     | `PolarLength`; defaults to zero                                |\n| `outerRadius`     | `PolarLength`; defaults to the layout radius                   |\n| `cornerRadius`    | D3 arc corner radius as a `PolarLength`                        |\n| `padRadius`       | Explicit D3 arc padding radius as a `PolarLength`              |\n| `generator`       | Responsive D3 arc factory for advanced per-datum geometry      |\n| `key`             | Stable arc identity; defaults to top/nested `id`, then index   |\n| `z`               | Geometry and interaction group                                 |\n| `color`           | Color-scale value; defaults to `z`                             |\n| `fill`            | Final constant or datum-derived paint override                 |\n| `fillOpacity`     | Fill opacity                                                   |\n| `stroke`          | Constant or datum-derived boundary stroke                      |\n| `strokeOpacity`   | Boundary opacity                                               |\n| `strokeWidth`     | Boundary width                                                 |\n| `strokeDasharray` | Boundary dash array                                            |\n| `opacity`         | Whole-arc opacity                                              |\n\nEach arc attaches its sampled painted boundary to its interaction point.\nDefault nearest focus therefore follows the visible slice, including holes,\nrounded corners, reversed sweeps, and custom D3 generators, instead of using\nonly the centroid anchor.\n\nUse the native `pie` transform for flat typed rows with source lineage. D3\n`pie` output remains valid interoperability input because its `startAngle`,\n`endAngle`, and `padAngle` fields are also the channels this mark needs. A pie,\ndonut, and gauge differ only in inner radius and angular interval.\n\n`generator` replaces the default D3 arc configuration for bespoke per-datum\ngeometry. Its factory receives the final `PolarLayoutContext`; keep the D3\ngenerator context `null` so it returns SVG path data. Standard hierarchy\npartitioning belongs to the optional\n[`sunburst`](./sunburst.md) mark, which accepts flat source rows and preserves\ntheir lineage.\n\n## `radialBarRadius` and `radialBarAngle`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialBarRadius<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialBarRadiusOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n\nfunction radialBarAngle<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialBarAngleOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n```\n\nThe two radial-bar marks transpose ordinary bar semantics across polar axes.\n`radialBarRadius` uses an angle band and a quantitative radius interval;\n`radialBarAngle` uses a radius band and a quantitative angle interval. The\ncategorical scale must have positive bandwidth. Configure spacing through the\nD3 band scale's inner and outer padding.\n\n| Mark              | Categorical channel             | Quantitative interval                                                   |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `radialBarRadius` | `angle`; defaults to row index  | `radius` is shorthand for `radius2`; `radius1` is the optional baseline |\n| `radialBarAngle`  | `radius`; defaults to row index | `angle` is shorthand for `angle2`; `angle1` is the optional baseline    |\n\nAn omitted `radialBarRadius.radius1` starts at physical radius zero, even when\n`PolarRadiusOptions.range` maps semantic zero to an inner offset. An explicit\n`radius1` is mapped through the radius scale. An omitted\n`radialBarAngle.angle1` is semantic zero and is mapped through the angle scale.\nUse the implicit physical-center radius baseline for nonnegative magnitudes.\nFor signed values or true radial intervals, set `radius1: 0` (or another\nsemantic baseline) so both endpoints map through the configured scale.\n\nBoth marks accept `id`, `className`, `angleScale`, `radiusScale`, `key`, `z`,\n`color`, `fill`, fill opacity, stroke styling, opacity, and motion.\n`cornerRadius` accepts a `PolarLength` or `\"full\"`; the latter resolves to half\nthe bar's radial thickness. Each valid bar emits one geometry-backed\ninteraction point at its quantitative endpoint and preserves its interval\nendpoints for focus and tooltip formatting.\n\n## `radialLine` and `radialArea`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialLine<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialLineOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n\nfunction radialArea<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialAreaOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n```\n\nBoth marks use `angle` and `radius` channels and accept `id`, `className`,\n`angleScale`, `radiusScale`, `key`, `z`, `color`, and a D3 curve factory. The\nchannels default to row index and a numeric datum. `color` contributes to the\nchart color scale and defaults to `z`. When `z` is omitted, an authored\n`color` also partitions the paths. When both are present, `z` remains the\nexplicit geometry and interaction group. `radialLine` accepts final stroke,\ndash, opacity, and optional `points` styling. `radialArea` accepts final fill\nand stroke styling plus `radius1` for an explicit inner scale value; `radius1`\ndefaults to zero.\n\nTheir datum key defaults to a unique top-level or nested `data.id`, then a\nunique angle within each effective path group, then row index.\n\nInput order is path order. Use a closed D3 curve such as\n`curveLinearClosed` for radar polygons. An explicit `z`, or `color` when `z`\nis absent, creates one path per group. `radialArea` can carry its own stroke;\nlayer a closed `radialLine` only when the outline needs independent styling.\n\n## `radialDot`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialDot<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialDotOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n```\n\n`radialDot` uses the same angle/radius channel defaults. It also accepts `id`,\n`className`, `angleScale`, `radiusScale`, `key`, `z`, `color`, `r`, `rScale`,\nfill, stroke, and opacity styling. Radius defaults to 3.5 pixels. Each valid\ndatum emits one interaction point with its original angle/radius values and\nprojected screen position. Its key defaults to a unique top-level or nested\n`data.id`, then row index.\n\n## `radialText`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialText<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialTextOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark<TDatum>\n```\n\n`radialText` maps `angle` and `radius` channels through the container's copied\npolar scales, then positions labels with D3's radial point projection. It\naccepts `angleScale`, `radiusScale`, `text`, `key`, `z`, `color`, fill, font\nsize and weight, anchor, baseline, rotation, and pixel `dx`/`dy`.\n`radiusOffset` is a signed constant or per-datum\nvisual channel applied in pixels after the semantic radius is mapped. It does\nnot contribute to the radius domain. Set `anchor: \"outside\"` to resolve\n`start`, `middle`, or `end` from the final mapped angle. Exact and near-exact\ntop and bottom angles use `middle`. A nonfinite resolved offset omits that\nlabel and its interaction point.\n\nUse it for arc labels, donut-center values, and gauge readouts without leaving\nthe polar coordinate system. Its interaction point follows the final radial\noffset plus `dx`/`dy` while retaining the original semantic radius value. Its\nkey defaults to a unique top-level or nested `data.id`, then row index.\n\n## `radialRule`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialRule<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: RadialRuleOptions<TDatum>,\n): PolarMark\n```\n\n`radialRule` emits one radial segment per datum. `angle`, `radius1`, and\n`radius2` are scale values; `radius1` defaults to zero. The mark also accepts\n`radius1Offset` and `radius2Offset` as signed constant or per-datum pixel\nvisual channels applied after the corresponding semantic radius is mapped.\nOffsets never contribute to radius-domain inference. A nonfinite resolved\nendpoint offset omits that segment. The mark also accepts `angleScale`,\n`radiusScale`, `key`, `z`, `color`, stroke, opacity, width, and dash styling.\nIt covers gauge needles, ticks, and pie-label leaders without expanding one\nlogical segment into two path rows. Rules remain decorative and emit no\ninteraction points. Its key defaults to a unique top-level or nested\n`data.id`, then a unique angle within each `z` group, then row index.\n\nPixel offsets do not reserve space outside the polar radius. Use\n`radiusRatio`, `inset`, or chart margins when labels or leaders must remain\ninside the chart surface.\n\n## `radialGrid` and `angleGrid`\n\n```ts\nfunction radialGrid(options?: RadialGridOptions): PolarGuide\nfunction angleGrid(options?: AngleGridOptions): PolarGuide\n```\n\n`radialGrid` draws radius values as circles or polygons. Its `scale` option\nselects a named radius scale, and `angleScale` selects the angle scale used for\npolygon rings. Supply explicit `values`, or let `ticks` request values from the\nselected radius scale. Labels are off by default. Label angle, offset,\nrotation, format, fill, and font size are configurable. Ring `fill` and\n`fillOpacity` can layer filled circle or polygon grids behind the chart marks.\n\n`angleGrid` draws spokes for explicit `values` or the selected angle domain.\nIts `scale` option selects a named angle scale. It can show labels around the\ncircumference with `format` and `labelOffset`. Labels are on by default and\nuse the same outside-anchor rule as\n`radialText({ anchor: \"outside\" })` unless `labelAnchor` is supplied. Both\nguides accept ID, class, stroke, opacity, width, and dash styling.\n\nGuide label position and orientation can be constants or callbacks through\n`PolarGuideLabelOption`. Each callback receives a `PolarGuideLabelContext`\nwith the semantic `value`, `index`, angle, radius, local x/y position, and\ncomplete layout. Use `labelAnchor`, `labelBaseline`, `labelDx`, `labelDy`, and\n`labelRotate` without rebuilding the guide. `labelClassName` targets the label\ngroup. Guides are decorative and emit no interaction points.\n\nEvery guide returns a `PolarGuideScene`:\n\n```ts\ninterface PolarGuideScene {\n  background: readonly SceneNode[]\n  foreground?: readonly SceneNode[]\n}\n```\n\n`polar` collects every guide background in declaration order, renders all\nmarks, then appends every optional foreground in the same guide order. The\nbuilt-in grids put rings and spokes in `background` and labels in\n`foreground`, keeping labels legible without painting grid geometry over the\ndata.\n\nThe exported option contracts are `PolarOptions`, `PolarScales`,\n`PolarPositionChannel`, `PolarPositionScaleOptions`, `RadialArcOptions`,\n`RadialBarRadiusOptions`, `RadialBarAngleOptions`, `RadialLineOptions`,\n`RadialAreaOptions`, `RadialDotOptions`, `RadialTextOptions`,\n`RadialRuleOptions`, `RadialGridOptions`, and `AngleGridOptions`. The coordinate contracts are `PolarAngleOptions`,\n`PolarRadiusOptions`, `PolarResolvedScale`, `PolarLayoutContext`,\n`PolarLength`, `PolarGuideLabelContext`, `PolarGuideLabelOption`, `PolarMark`,\n`PolarGuide`, and `PolarGuideScene`. `PolarMark` and `PolarGuide` annotate\nbuilt-in constructor results rather than a supported custom-extension\nboundary.\n\nSee [Polar and Radar Charts](../../examples/polar-and-radar.md) for pie,\ndonut, gauge, radar, radial bar, numeric line, and numeric scatter\ncompositions.\n"}
